4 Background information
------------------------

This patch is based off the 2cc Trains in NML set by Transportman 
and the 2cc Narrow-Gauge in NML set by althonos. The goal is to
bring consistency with the 2cc Narrow-Gauge in NML set.

4.1 Representation
------------------

Similarly to the original 2cc Trains in NML set, the goal is to
bring a broad representation of the trains of the world rather
than necessarily assigning each engine to their most common operator. 
For instance, the ``GE C30-7`` has been delivered in 305 units to the 
National Railways of Mexico and in 2 units to the Ulaanbaatar Railway,
yet, since Mongolia has no other locomotives available (so far),
it receives the ``GE C30-7``.

4.2 History / Geography
-----------------------

The country assignment refers to the modern-times country occupying the
geographic region where the train was running. For instance, a locomotive
operating in Silesia under Prussian administration would be assigned 
to Poland rather than Germany.

4.3 Naming
----------

Operator name should be the name *at introduction date*; for instance, pre-1975
locomotives from Western Australia are classified under the reporting mark ``WAGR``
(Western Australian Government Railways); post-1975 under the mark ``Westrail`` 
following the name change.

If possible, class designation is used (e.g. ``DB 182``), and if not the company
running numbers with a prefix (e.g. ``Oriental Railway No. 12-20``). Unique 
locomotives or series of vehicles without a consistent numbering scheme may be
Engine naming follows the operator class and rolling number assignment rather than
the manufacturer designation. For *relatively* widespread manufacturer names that are 
used commonly instead of the operator designation, the name *may* be added as a nickname
(e.g. ``DB 642 'Desiro'`` for the Siemens Desiro DMUs).

The numbering describes the whole range, *unless* one of the locomotives has 
outstanding qualities (e.g. a specific nickname, or is conserved in a museum); in which 
case, the single locomotive may be used to name the whole class (e.g. ``AGL No. 31 'Perruca'``, 
which has sister locomotives No. 29 and No. 30; however only No. 31 was nicknamed Perruca,
and it is the only surviving member of that class, being conserved at the 
Museo Nacional Ferroviario in Spain). 


